blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:160:\begin{remark}[The printed attainment form of the symmetrization lemma]\label{rem:symmetric-strat-limit}
blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:161:	The source prints Lemma~\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} in the following stronger form (\path{references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex}, lines~94--99): if $\mathfrak G$ is a symmetric game with $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G) = 1-\eps$ for some $\eps \ge 0$, then there exists a symmetric and projective strategy $\mathcal S = (\ket{\psi}, M)$ with $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G, \mathcal S) \ge 1-\eps$. This development does not assert that sentence anywhere. It is recorded verbatim, and unasserted, as the Lean proposition \texttt{PrintedSymmetricProjectiveAttainmentClaim}.
blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:163:	Two independent defects separate it from the corrected statement. First, the argument of Lemma~\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} yields, for each $\eps' > \eps$, a symmetric projective strategy of value at least $1-\eps'$, and the conclusion at $\eps' = \eps$ does not follow: the dilated dimension $d$ may grow as $\eps' \downarrow \eps$, and the supremum defining $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G)$ in Definition~\ref{def:tensor-product-value} need not be attained by any strategy. Second, on the formal domain the printed sentence is false: Definition~\ref{def:symmetric-game} permits an empty answer alphabet, for which no strategy exists at all while $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G)$ is still defined as the totalized supremum of the empty set, so the hypotheses hold at $\eps = 1$ and the conclusion has no witness. The Lean declaration \texttt{not\_forall\_printed\-Symmetric\-Projective\-Attainment\-Claim} proves exactly this refutation of the universally quantified printed claim.
blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:168:	Lemma~\ref{lem:symmetric-strat-given-strategy}. Together with arbitrary
blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:205:	\label{lem:symmetric-strat-printed-claim}
blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:210:	This formalization-only node is not a named statement of the paper. It records the printed sentence of Remark~\ref{rem:symmetric-strat-limit} as a proposition $\mathrm{PrintedAttainment}(\mathfrak G, \eps)$ --- namely, ``$\eps \ge 0$ and $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G) = 1-\eps$ imply that some symmetric projective strategy $\mathcal S$ has $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G, \mathcal S) \ge 1-\eps$'' --- without asserting it, and states its refutation: it is \emph{not} the case that $\mathrm{PrintedAttainment}(\mathfrak G, \eps)$ holds for every symmetric game $\mathfrak G$ and every real $\eps$.
blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:215:	Take $\mathfrak G$ with one question, empty answer alphabet, the uniform distribution on the single question pair, and the unique decision predicate on the empty answer domain; both symmetry conditions hold. A unit vector in $\hilb_A \ot \hilb_B$ forces both local index types to be nonempty, while completeness of an empty measurement on a nonzero space would equate $0$ with the identity, so $\mathfrak G$ has no strategy at all. The set of strategy values is therefore empty and $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G) = 0$ by the totalization convention for the real conditional supremum of the empty set. At $\eps = 1$ both hypotheses of $\mathrm{PrintedAttainment}(\mathfrak G, 1)$ hold and its conclusion has no witness. This refutes the printed claim over the formal domain only; it settles nothing about attainment for symmetric games with nonempty answer alphabets, for which the unattained-supremum obstruction of Remark~\ref{rem:symmetric-strat-limit} is the operative one. See \cite{gap:qpbt_symmetrization-attainment}.
